The digital transformation of dentistry has led to the increasing use of imaging data in diagnosis, treatment planning, intervention, and follow-up~\cite{Horner2012,Kapila2015,Assiri2020,Chogle2020}. Periapical and panoramic radiographs, cone-beam computed tomography (CBCT), intraoral scans, and intraoral photographs now play a central role in contemporary dental care and research~\cite{Horner2012,Kapila2015,Shujaat2021,Lahoud2022}. In parallel, artificial intelligence (AI), particularly deep learning-based computer vision, has shown considerable promise for automating clinically relevant tasks such as anatomical segmentation, lesion detection, tooth identification, disease classification, and outcome prediction~\cite{Schwendicke2020,Wang2021,Shaheen2021,Cui2022,Yeshua2023,Fu2024}. These advances have raised expectations that dental AI may improve diagnostic consistency, reduce clinician workload, and support precision dentistry at scale~\cite{Schwendicke2020,Schwendicke2022BigData}.

However, the translation of dental AI from proof-of-concept studies to reproducible research workflows remains limitation. The bottleneck is often not the lack of capable models, but the fragmented and technically demanding workflow required to use them reliably~\cite{Schwendicke2021Checklist,Polizzi2023,Dot2024DentalSegmentator}. Dental AI experiments typically involve heterogeneous data ingestion, annotation harmonization, case structuring, dataset partitioning, preprocessing, model configuration, metric calculation, and result reporting. These steps are often implemented through task-specific scripts or locally improvised pipelines, making results difficult to reproduce, compare, and audit across studies.

This problem is amplified by the heterogeneity of dental imaging data. Differences in acquisition devices, file formats, voxel spacing, image quality, field of view, and artifact profiles are common across centres and modalities~\cite{Nemtoi2013,Shujaat2021,Dot2024DentalSegmentator}. Annotation practices also vary in naming conventions, anatomical granularity, and quality control procedures~\cite{Polizzi2023,Zheng2024,Schwendicke2021Checklist}. In addition, inappropriate dataset splitting, including patient-level leakage or insufficient separation between internal and external validation cohorts, can lead to overoptimistic performance estimates~\cite{Schwendicke2021Checklist,Polizzi2023}. These issues increase the technical burden for dental researchers and weaken the reliability of downstream AI experiments.

Existing solutions address only part of this workflow problem. Some studies have released pretrained models or task-specific software tools for selected dental imaging applications~\cite{Shaheen2021,Cui2022,Dot2024DentalSegmentator}, while general medical imaging and machine learning platforms can support individual stages of model development~\cite{Isensee2021nnUNet,Fedorov2012Slicer,Wasserthal2023}. Nevertheless, task-specific tools often have limited generalizability across modalities and datasets, whereas general-purpose platforms may require substantial computational expertise and do not necessarily provide dental-specific data governance, leakage-aware splitting, standardized evaluation, or clinician-readable reporting.

To address this gap, we developed DentalClaw, a natural-language-driven and auditable workflow platform for dental imaging AI research. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:system_overview}, DentalClaw is organized around data curation, experiment execution, and structured reporting. The platform does not aim to introduce a new segmentation backbone. Instead, it aims to convert user research intent into standardized, executable, and reviewable workflows, while delegating data processing, model execution, evaluation, and logging to deterministic modules.

The main aim of this study was to propose and evaluate DentalClaw, a natural-language-driven platform for standardized dental imaging AI workflows. The platform was evaluated on representative 2D panoramic radiograph and 3D CBCT segmentation tasks, with a focus on workflow completion, automation efficiency, reproducibility, and structured reporting.
