\subsection{Expert-informed configuration trials across imaging modalities}

To further evaluate whether DentalClaw could encode and reuse clinical-grade model-configuration knowledge within a traceable workflow, we conducted skill-based configuration studies on both 2D panoramic radiographs (TDD dataset) and 3D CBCT volumes (ToothFairy dataset). These trials examined how DentalClaw's modular orchestration of ``skills'' can lower technical barriers while achieving competitive performance within the evaluated workflows.

\subsubsection{Trajectory validation and user comparison on 2D panoramic radiographs}

In the TDD-derived 32-class panoramic segmentation task, we assessed the platform's ability to orchestrate a progression of expert skills. These skills were inspired by recurring practices in large-scale medical image analysis competitions \cite{wang2024stsmiccai2023challenge, wang2025miccaists2024challenge}. Starting from a U-Mamba baseline, DentalClaw sequentially invoked these reusable skills to refine the experimental plan under the same audit and logging framework. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:ablation}, the performance improved from a Mean Dice of 0.6163 to 0.6937, with Mean HD95 reducing from 83.15~mm to 38.54~mm.

To highlight the platform's utility in bridging the expertise gap, we compared the automated results with configurations manually selected by three users with varying technical backgrounds (Student A, Student B, and an AI researcher). As summarized in Table~\ref{tab:expertise}, user-selected configurations yielded diverse outcomes; however, the DentalClaw skill-based workflow (Exp6) outperformed all manual configurations, including those of the AI researcher. Figure~\ref{fig:expertise_masks} provides qualitative evidence that DentalClaw's automated orchestration produces cleaner boundaries and more consistent tooth numbering, demonstrating that pre-encoded expert knowledge can effectively substitute for intensive manual trial-and-error.

