In this workflow, DentalClaw executed three predefined segmentation modules with different architectural characteristics: nnU-Net\cite{Isensee2021} as a CNN-based module, SwinUNETR \cite{Hatamizadeh2022SwinUNETR} as a Transformer-based module, and U-MambaBot \cite{Ma2024UMamba} as an SSM-based module. The platform then applied predefined multi-model ensemble \cite{Ju2018EnsembleCNN} and post-processing skills, including majority voting, mean-softmax ensembling, test-time augmentation (TTA)~\cite{AutomaticBrain, Moshkov2020TTA}, and anatomy-guided refinement. All intermediate predictions, selected configurations, fusion outputs, post-processing records, and evaluation metrics were stored as part of the same workflow trace.

As shown in Table~\ref{tab:cbct_trajectory}, the individual modules showed different performance patterns across natural teeth and metallic restorations. The nnU-Net baseline achieved a Mean Dice of 0.7038 for natural teeth, whereas SwinUNETR and U-MambaBot showed higher Dice values for restorations. After platform-managed fusion and anatomy-guided refinement, the final DentalClaw-orchestrated output achieved a Mean Dice of 0.8058 for natural teeth and 0.7653 for restorations, with the Mean HD95 for natural teeth decreasing from 10.386~mm in the nnU-Net baseline to 4.528~mm. Qualitative examples in Fig.~\ref{fig:cbct_qualitative_comparison} further illustrate that the fused output produced smoother surfaces and fewer visible label inconsistencies in the representative cases.

These results indicate that DentalClaw can package multi-model 3D segmentation procedures into reproducible workflow skills, including model execution, fusion, post-processing, metric aggregation, and qualitative result generation. The experiment should be interpreted as a workflow orchestration case study rather than as a standalone comparison of new CBCT segmentation algorithms.



\subsection{Private object detection workflow and lesion-focused refinement}

To further evaluate DentalClaw on private clinical data, we conducted an object detection workflow using our own dataset of 290 dental radiographs. The dataset was annotated for four target categories: pulp, root canal fillings, fillings, and periapical lesion. DentalClaw standardized the annotation format, checked image--annotation consistency, generated a fixed train--validation--test split, and executed the detection workflow with traceable training and evaluation records. The split contained 203 training images, 58 validation images, and 29 test images. For periapical lesions, the corresponding numbers of lesion instances were 66, 19, and 10, respectively.

In the initial four-class detection experiment, DentalClaw trained and evaluated a detector on the validation set. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:private_detection_four_class}, the overall mAP$_{50}$ was 0.613 and the mAP$_{50-95}$ was 0.335. The detector achieved relatively higher performance for pulp, root canal fillings, and fillings. In contrast, periapical lesion detection showed substantially lower performance, with a precision of 0.208, recall of 0.071, mAP$_{50}$ of 0.076, and mAP$_{50-95}$ of 0.025. This result indicated that the lesion category was the main bottleneck in the private detection workflow.

