\section{Discussion}

DentalClaw as a natural-language-driven workflow platform for dental imaging AI research. The results suggest that DentalClaw can convert user research instructions into executable workflows covering dataset preparation, model training, inference, evaluation, and structured reporting. Importantly, the contribution of DentalClaw should be interpreted as workflow infrastructure rather than as a new segmentation model. Its value lies in organizing fragmented, operator-dependent steps into a standardized and reviewable process for dental AI experimentation.

DentalClaw completed end-to-end workflows in two representative but methodologically different scenarios: 2D panoramic radiograph segmentation and 3D CBCT segmentation. These tasks differ in data dimensionality, file organization, preprocessing requirements, and model execution logic. The ability to handle both workflows under a shared platform structure suggests that a case-level data representation, combined with task-specific execution modules, can support standardized AI experimentation across heterogeneous dental imaging modalities.

The platform also reduced manual coordination burden compared with manually assembled scripts while preserving comparable task-level segmentation performance. This finding is relevant because many practical barriers in dental AI research arise not from the absence of capable models, but from the effort required to prepare datasets, configure experiments, check data quality, run evaluation, and organize outputs reproducibly. From this perspective, DentalClaw provides a workflow standardization layer that may improve procedural efficiency and reproducibility in research settings. This interpretation is consistent with recent calls for more transparent and methodologically controlled dental AI studies~\cite{Uribe2025}.

Another practical feature of DentalClaw is its structured reporting layer. Instead of returning only model checkpoints, raw predictions, or console logs, the platform generated reports containing workflow metadata, preparation summaries, quantitative results, prediction files, and case-level review information. These outputs may help researchers inspect how an experiment was configured, what data were used, and how results were produced. However, this component was evaluated only as a research reporting and traceability tool. It should not be interpreted as a validated clinical decision-support interface.

Several limitations should be noted. First, although an exploratory private object-detection workflow was included, the main validation focused on representative segmentation workflows, and broader tasks such as classification, registration, multimodal fusion, and case-level decision support were not systematically evaluated. Second, although DentalClaw was designed to reduce the technical burden for dental researchers, this study did not include a formal usability study with dentists or clinical researchers. Third, the reporting component was not formally evaluated in terms of readability, usefulness, or review efficiency. Fourth, the task-level experiments were intended to demonstrate workflow feasibility rather than state-of-the-art segmentation performance across diverse external cohorts.

Future work should evaluate DentalClaw on a broader range of dental imaging tasks, datasets, and institutional settings. Formal user studies are also needed to determine whether natural-language-driven workflow interaction reduces setup time, improves workflow comprehension, and enables more independent participation by dental researchers. In addition, the data preparation and reporting layers could be extended with stronger annotation auditing, leakage analysis, metadata harmonization, and interactive review functions. Any transition from research workflow support to clinical use would require prospective validation, human oversight, and regulatory consideration.



\section{Conclusions}
DentalClaw demonstrated the feasibility of natural-language-guided orchestration for standardized dental imaging AI workflows. By integrating dataset ingestion, quality auditing, model execution, evaluation, and structured reporting into a traceable workflow, the platform reduced manual coordination burden and improved the reproducibility and reviewability of dental AI experimentation. These findings support DentalClaw as a practical research infrastructure for assisting dentists and clinical researchers in conducting more standardized imaging AI studies. Future work will extend the platform to broader dental imaging tasks, multi-center datasets, and more interactive user-facing research scenarios.

